Specifically, with $\{d_i\}$, a tangent sequence w.r.t. $\F_i = \sigma(d_1,...,d_i)$ can be obtained via the following steps:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep, topsep=0pt]
    \item First, we take $d_1$ and $e_1$ to be two independent copies of the same random mechanism. 
    \item With ($d_1,..,d_{i-1}$), the i-th pair of variables $d_i$ and $e_i$ comes from i.i.d. copies of the same random mechanism given $\F_{i-1}$. 
\end{itemize}
And $e_i$’s are conditionally independent w.r.t. $\mathcal{G} = \F_n$.

An example of constructing tangent sequence can be found in section \ref{sec: Applications}. Here we provide another example using (generalized) U-statistic. A generalized U-statistic $U_n$ of $n$ independent random variable $X_1,...,X_n \in \mathcal X$ with a class of functions $f_{i,j}: \mathcal X\times \mathcal X \rightarrow \mathbb R$, $1\leq i<j\leq n$, is defined as   
\begin{equation}
    \label{equ: generalized U stats}
    U_n= \sum\limits_{1\leq i<j\leq n}  f_{i,j}(X_i,X_j).
\end{equation}
When $f_{i,j}(\cdot,\cdot) \equiv f(\cdot,\cdot) := \frac{2}{n(n-1)} h(\cdot,\cdot)$ and $X_i$'s are i.i.d., $U_n$ becomes the U-statistic with kernel $f$. A classical way to handle the variance is Hoeffding decomposition (see e.g., Chapters 11 \& 12 \cite{MR1652247}), while decoupling considers the natural filtration $\mathbb F = \{\F_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq n}$, where $\F_i = \sigma(\{X_1,...,X_i\})$. With $d_j : = \sum\limits_{i=1}^{j-1} f_{i,j}(X_i, X_j)$, we rewrite $U_n : = \sum\limits_{j=2}^n d_j$. The decoupled sequence $e_j: = \sum\limits_{i=1}^{j-1} f_{i,j}(X_i,\tX_j)  $ is then constructed, with $\tX_i$ the independent copy of $X_i$ for each $i$. As an instance of generalized U-statistics, the quadratic form $Q_n = \sum\limits_{1\leq i<j\leq n}  a_{ij} X_i X_j$ has the corresponding decoupled sum $\sum\limits_{j=2}^n \left( \sum\limits_{i=1}^{j-1} a_{ij} X_i \right)\tilde{X}_j$. With the decoupling inequalities in Section \ref{sec: Main results}, we can use the variance (or the second moment) of the decoupled sum to bound the variance (or the second moment) of $U_n$. Similarly to the technique in section \ref{sec: Applications}, we can also derive an upper bound for the variance (or the second moment) of $U_\tau$, for some $\mathbb F$-stopping time $\tau$.

