post_theorem_intro_text:
We state a simple observation which empasize the prominent role of the supremum norm later on.
\begin{rem}\label{npn}
    With the notation from Definition \ref{npd} the following inequality holds true:
    \begin{equation*}
        \|(a_{1},\ldots,a_{n})\|_{NP(A;\varphi_{1},\ldots,\varphi_{n})}\geq\|(a_{1},\ldots,a_{n})\|_{\infty}
    \end{equation*}
\end{rem}
Indeed, fix $\varepsilon>0$ and find $x\in A$ with $\widehat{x}(\varphi_{i})=a_{i}$ for all $i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$ such that 
\begin{equation*}
     \|(a_{1},\ldots,a_{n})\|_{NP(A;\varphi_{1},\ldots,\varphi_{n})}>\|x\|_{A}-\varepsilon.
\end{equation*}
Since the norm in the Banach algebra is larger then the spectral norm, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
    \|x\|_{A}\geq\|\widehat{x}\|_{C(\triangle(A))}\geq\max\{|\widehat{x}(\varphi_{1})|,\ldots,|\widehat{x}(\varphi_{n})|\}=\|(a_{1},\ldots,a_{n})\|_{\infty},
\end{equation*}
which finishes the argument.
\\
Let us continue the discussion with the explanation of the title. Let $H^{\infty}(\mathbb{D})$ be the space of all bounded analytic functions on the unit disc. Then $\mathbb{D}\subset\triangle(H^{\infty}(\mathbb{D}))$ via point evaluations. The classical Nevanlinna-Pick theorem (for related results, check \cite{s}) asserts that for a given data $\lambda_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{n},z_{1},\ldots,z_{n}\in\mathbb{D}$ there exists $f\in H^{\infty}(\mathbb{D})$ with $\|f\|_{\infty}\leq t$ such that $f(\lambda_{i})=z_{i}$ for all $i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$ if and only if the Pick matrix
\begin{equation}\label{NPc}
    \left(\frac{1-t^{-2}
    \overline{z_{j}}z_{i}}{1-\overline{\lambda_{j}}\lambda_{i}}\right)_{i,j=1}^{n}\text{ is positive semi-definite.}
\end{equation}
We can refer to the Nevalinna-Pick theorem in the following way. Let $A=H^{\infty}(\mathbb{D})$ and let $\varphi_{1},\ldots,\varphi_{n}$ be point-evaluations at the distinct points $\lambda_{1},\ldots,\lambda_{n}$ (respectively). Then for any sequence $(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})\in\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\begin{equation*}
    \|(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})\|_{NP(H^{\infty}(\mathbb{D});\varphi_{1},\ldots,\varphi_{n})}=
    \inf\{t>0: \text{the condition \ref{NPc} holds}\}.
\end{equation*}
In view of Remark \ref{npn} the Nevalinna-Pick theorem gives a necessary and sufficient condition for the Nevanlinna-Pick norm to be minimal for the algebra $H^{\infty}(\mathbb{D})$.

