\label{thm: main}
Let $(M,g)$ be a four-dimensional spacetime satisfying the null energy condition. 
 Assume there is a smooth spacelike compact Cauchy surface $V$ which is expanding in all directions (i.e., its second fundamental form $K$ is positive definite). Then either 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $V$ is a spherical space (i.e., it is topologically a quotient of $S^3$), or
\item[(ii)] $M$ is past null geodesically incomplete. 
\end{itemize}

