expanded_sketch:
We do not prove this result directly, but instead prove two different stronger results, each implying the main theorem.

Both proofs share the idea to use an automaton to study an approximation where one only forbids cubes of length at most $p$. Since short cubes are more difficult to avoid than long cubes, taking $p$ large should give a good approximation; then, using standard tools from combinatorics on words and automata theory, studying this approximation is quite simple. The delicate part is to show that for $p$ large enough, this approximation is good enough to say something about the original problem where *all* cubes are forbidden.

First route (via growth): show that the growth rate of the language is not far from the growth rate of the regular approximation of the language. We first prove the following theorem. \begin{theorem} \label{thm:growth}  
For any sequence of binary alphabet $\A_1,\A_2,\dots$, and for every $n$, there exist at least $1.35^n$ cube-free words in $\A_1\times\A_2\times\dots\times\A_n$. 
\end{theorem}
This uses the technique (Kolpakov/Shur, adapted in Rosenfeld, “Avoiding …” (2022)) to deduce bounds on the growth of a power-free language from its approximation.

Second route (via constructivity): again compute properties of the same automaton, but then use a different argument for adding the large cubes back; namely, introduce a weight function that measures how difficult it is to extend a word, and prove that under a condition, if the weight is small then at least one of the extensions also has a small weight, enabling an inductive construction. We first prove the following theorem. \begin{theorem} \label{thm:computable}
Let $\A_1,\A_2,\dots$ be a computable sequence of binary alphabet. Then there exists a computable infinite cube-free word $w=w_1w_2w_3\ldots$ in $\A_1\times\A_2\times\dots$. Moreover, there is an algorithm that given $w_1\ldots w_n$ and\ $\A_{n+1}$ computes $w_{n+1}$ in time polynomial in $n$.
\end{theorem}
This follows the approach of Miller, generalized in Rosenfeld (2025).

Finally, the theorem above and the preceding theorem both independently imply the main theorem.

